'''Liam Jared Lawson''' (born 11 February 2002) is a New Zealand [[racing driver]] who competes in [[Formula One]] for [[Racing Bulls]].

Born in [[Hastings, New Zealand|Hastings]] and raised in [[Pukekohe]], Lawson began competitive [[kart racing]] aged six. Lawson, who is mentored by three-time [[New Zealand Grand Prix]] winner [[Ken Smith (racing driver)|Ken Smith]], graduated to [[junior formulae]] in 2015, winning his first title in the [[New Zealand Formula Ford Championship]] as a [[privateer (motorsport)|privateer]]. He finished runner-up in the [[2017 Australian Formula 4 Championship|2017 Australian F4]], [[2018 ADAC Formula 4 Championship|2018 ADAC F4]] and [[2019 Euroformula Open Championship|2019 Euroformula Open]] championships, before winning the [[Toyota Racing Series]] in [[2019 Toyota Racing Series|2019]] with [[M2 Competition|M2]]. Lawson then progressed to [[FIA Formula 3]] in {{FIAF3|2020}} before moving to [[FIA Formula 2]] in [[2021 Formula 2 Championship|2021]], where he placed third the [[2022 Formula 2 Championship|following season]] with [[Carlin Motorsport|Carlin]]. He also competed in the [[2021 Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters]] for [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]] [[AF Corse]] alongside [[Alex Albon]], finishing runner-up to [[Maximilian Götz]] amidst a controversial finale. Lawson then competed in the [[2023 Super Formula Championship]], finishing runner-up to [[Ritomo Miyata]] with [[Team Mugen|Mugen]].

A member of the [[Red Bull Junior Team]] since 2019, Lawson was a reserve driver for both [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]] and [[Scuderia AlphaTauri|AlphaTauri]] from {{F1|2022}} to {{F1|2024}}. Lawson made his Formula One debut at the [[2023 Dutch Grand Prix]], replacing an injured [[Daniel Ricciardo]] at AlphaTauri for five Grands Prix in {{F1|2023}}, scoring his maiden points finish in [[2023 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore]]. He replaced Ricciardo full-time at the re-branded [[Racing Bulls]] in {{F1|2024}} from the {{F1GP|2024|United States}} onwards. Lawson was promoted to a full-time drive with parent team [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]] for his {{F1|2025}} campaign—replacing [[Sergio Pérez]] to partner [[Max Verstappen]]—but was demoted after the [[2025 Chinese Grand Prix|second round]]. Lawson is contracted to remain at Racing Bulls until at least the end of the 2026 season.

== Early and personal life ==
Liam Jared Lawson was born on 11 February 2002 in [[Hastings, New Zealand|Hastings]], New Zealand.<ref name="RB2025">{{Cite web |title=Lawson confirmed as Verstappen's Red Bull team mate for 2025 |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/breaking-lawson-confirmed-as-verstappens-red-bull-team-mate-for-2025.4umEL3YzqWIv9JEV7qI2Gn |access-date=19 December 2024 |date=19 December 2024 |website=[[Formula 1]] |publisher=[[Formula One Group]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250223050803/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/breaking-lawson-confirmed-as-verstappens-red-bull-team-mate-for-2025.4umEL3YzqWIv9JEV7qI2Gn |archive-date=23 February 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.statsf1.com/fr/liam-lawson.aspx |title=Liam Lawson |website=Stats F1 |language=fr |access-date=26 October 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251026123311/https://www.statsf1.com/fr/liam-lawson.aspx |archive-date=26 October 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He was raised in [[Pukekohe]], which was home to [[Pukekohe Park Raceway]], located in the [[Auckland Region]] of the [[North Island]].<ref name="MS24">{{Cite web |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/liam-lawsons-rocky-road-to-f1-my-parents-sold-their-house-so-i-could-keep-racing/10658101/ |title=Liam Lawson's rocky road to F1: 'My parents sold their house so I could keep racing' |last=Cleeren |first=Filip |date=26 September 2024 |website=[[Motorsport.com]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=19 December 2024}}</ref> His parents sold their house to fund his racing career.<ref name="MS24"/>

Lawson selected the number 30 as his personal driver number in Formula One, which he had used since age eight in honour of his karting mentor.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.planetf1.com/news/liam-lawson-f1-race-number-30-reasons |title=Liam Lawson reveals karting 'hero' inspiration behind choice of F1 race number |author=Henry Valantine |website=Planet F1 |date=11 October 2024}}</ref> He plays the [[guitar]] and has recorded music.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nowtolove.co.nz/celebrity/celeb-news/liam-lawson-fame/ |title=Liam's on the road to success |website=Now to Love NZ |date=16 July 2024}}</ref> He went to the same school in [[Waiau Pa]] with Supercars driver [[Matt Payne]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Kiwi connection: Payne's ties to F1 debutant Lawson |url=https://www.supercars.com/news/kiwi-connection-matt-paynes-ties-to-f1-debutant-liam-lawson |work=Supercars |date=26 August 2023}}</ref>

== Junior racing career ==
=== Karting ===
Lawson began karting in 2008, competing in numerous championships across New Zealand, including two karting titles in 2014. Each year, he returned to the Kartsport Auckland Go Kart Club on [[Rosebank, Auckland|Rosebank Road]], [[Avondale, Auckland|Avondale]] and competed in the big City of Sails race on [[Auckland Anniversary Day|Auckland Anniversary Weekend]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://juniorteam.redbull.com/athlete/liam-lawson|title=Liam Lawson |publisher=Red Bull Junior Team|access-date=18 February 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/waikato-times/sport/68942052/Pukekohes-Liam-Lawson-wins-50-000-SpeedSport-Scholarship|title=Pukekohe's Liam Lawson wins $50,000 SpeedSport Scholarship|date=29 May 2015|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kartsport.org.nz/about-us/latest-news/two-in-one-final-for-iks-three-way|title=Two-in-one Final for IKS Three-way|date=2014|access-date=24 November 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190926171246/http://www.kartsport.org.nz/about-us/latest-news/two-in-one-final-for-iks-three-way|archive-date=26 September 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://ekartingnews.com/2014/07/15/2014-kartsport-new-zealand-national-schools-championship/|title=2014 KartSport New Zealand National Schools' Championship|date=15 July 2014|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.pressreader.com/new-zealand/weekend-herald/20180623/283265983536244|title=Foot on the pedal, an eye on the prize|date=23 June 2018|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=POINTS KZ2/KZ2 Heavy Points, City Of Sails 2022, KartSport Auckland, New Zealand - Official Event Results, MYLAPS Speedhive |url=https://speedhive.mylaps.com/Sessions/6415018 |access-date=22 October 2022 |website=speedhive.mylaps.com}}</ref>

=== Lower formulae ===
In 2015, Lawson made his single-seater debut in the Formula First Manfeild Winter Series with [[Sabre Motorsport]], taking a win and ten podiums to finish second overall.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson Ready for Championship Debut|url=http://www.speedsportscholarship.co.nz/|access-date=24 November 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160526231126/http://www.speedsportscholarship.co.nz/lawson-ready-for-championship-debut/|archive-date=26 May 2016 |url-status=dead}}</ref> A few months later, he joined Sabre to contest the [[New Zealand Formula First Championship|NZ Formula First]] championship, taking a win and three podiums on his way to sixth in the championship and the Rookie of the Year title.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.sabremotorsport.co.nz/lawson-leading-rookie-after-championship-debut/|title=Lawson Leading Rookie after Championship Debut|date=9 October 2015|access-date=24 November 2018|archive-date=29 February 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200229104557/http://www.sabremotorsport.co.nz/lawson-leading-rookie-after-championship-debut/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.speedsportscholarship.co.nz/lawson-set-for-hometown-debut-after-maiden-formula-first-win/|title=Lawson Set for Hometown Debut after Maiden Formula First Win|date=8 December 2015|access-date=24 November 2018}}{{Dead link|date=April 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/waikato-times/sport/78697400/pukekohes-liam-lawson-wins-rookie-of-the-year-at-nz-formula-first-championship|title=Pukekohe's Liam Lawson wins Rookie of the Year at NZ Formula First championship|date=8 April 2016|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref> The following year, Lawson graduated to the [[New Zealand Formula Ford Championship|NZ F1600 Championship Series]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thepost.nz/articles/liam-lawson-stepping-up-to-formula-ford/|title=Liam Lawson Stepping Up To Formula Ford|date=24 May 2016|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref> There he dominated proceedings, claiming fourteen of the fifteen victories on offer to become the youngest champion in not just the series' history, but the youngest ever Formula Ford champion in world at the time.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://nzmotorracing.co.nz/2017/02/15/youngest-formula-1600-champion-liam-lawson/|title=Youngest Formula 1600 champion – Liam Lawson|date=15 February 2017|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref>

In 2017, Lawson moved up to the [[2017 Australian Formula 4 Championship|Australian F4 championship]] with [[Team BRM|BRM]], taking five wins to finish second  in only his rookie season.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.theracetorque.com/2017/06/5-open-wheel-young-guns-you-should-be-watching/|title=5 OPEN WHEEL YOUNG GUNS YOU SHOULD BE WATCHING|date=5 June 2017|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://nzmotorracing.co.nz/2017/04/08/liam-lawson-wins-opening-australia-f4-round/|title=Liam Lawson wins opening Australia F4 round|date=8 April 2017|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref> The following year, Lawson remained at Formula 4 level, moving across to contest the [[2018 ADAC Formula 4 Championship|ADAC Formula 4 championship]] with [[Van Amersfoort Racing]] and received backing from Turner's, the New Zealand used car network that had previously sponsored [[IndyCar]] champion [[Scott Dixon]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.adac-motorsport.de/adac-formel-4/uk/artikel/liam-lawson-joins-van-amersfoort-racing-rising-new-zealand-race-driver-continues-his-career-in-europe--245365|title=Liam Lawson joins Van Amersfoort Racing|date=19 March 2018|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.turners.co.nz/liam-lawson/|title=Turners & Liam Lawson Sponsorship|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref> Claiming three wins and three pole positions, Lawson's performances saw him claim his second consecutive F4 runner up position, behind [[Lirim Zendeli]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thepost.nz/articles/pukekohes-lawson-claims-runner-up-in-german-f4-championship/|title=Pukekohe's Lawson claims runner-up in German F4 championship|date=2 October 2018|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref>

=== Toyota Racing Series ===
In November 2018, Lawson joined [[M2 Competition (team)|M2 Competition]] for the [[2019 Toyota Racing Series|2019 championship]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.formulascout.com/liam-lawson-gets-toyota-racing-series-seat-with-m2-competition/46756|title=Liam Lawson gets Toyota Racing Series seat with M2 Competition|last=Allen|first=Peter|work=FormulaScout|date=28 November 2018|access-date=29 November 2018}}</ref> Lawson dominated on debut at Highlands, taking two races wins by over nine-seconds each and won the Dorothy Smith Memorial Trophy as a result of winning Race 3.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.driven.co.nz/news/motorsport/kiwi-1-2-liam-lawson-claims-toyota-racing-series-win-on-debut/ | title=Kiwi 1–2: Liam Lawson claims Toyota Racing Series win on debut }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|author=Rotorua Daily Post |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/news/article.cfm?c_id=4&objectid=12189716 |title=TRS: Liam Lawson celebrates 'biggest win of his career' |publisher=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=13 January 2019 |accessdate=8 May 2022}}</ref> Claiming three additional wins across the season, Lawson secured the title at the [[2019 New Zealand Grand Prix|New Zealand Grand Prix]] after a season long battle with [[Ferrari Driver Academy|Ferrari junior]] and fellow countryman [[Marcus Armstrong]].<ref name="TRS">{{cite web|url=http://www.formulascout.com/lawson-wins-new-zealand-grand-prix/48154|title=Lawson defeats Armstrong in New Zealand GP to secure TRS title|last=Waring|first=Bethonie|work=FormulaScout|date=10 February 2019|access-date=10 February 2019}}</ref>

=== Euroformula Open Championship ===
Lawson had been set to join the inaugural season of the Formula European Masters with [[Motopark Academy|Motopark]], alongside fellow Red Bull Junior [[Yuki Tsunoda]], but followed the German outfit to the [[2019 Euroformula Open Championship|Euroformula Open Championship]] when Formula European Masters was cancelled due to a lack of entrants.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.formulascout.com/liam-lawson-signed-to-red-bull-junior-team-after-trs-title-to-race-for-motopark-in-fem/48386|title=Liam Lawson signed to Red Bull Junior Team after TRS title, to race for Motopark in FEM|last=Allen|first=Peter|work=Formula Scout|date=18 February 2019|access-date=18 February 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.formulascout.com/motopark-to-euroformula-open/49330|title=Motopark makes Euroformula Open switch, retains FEM line-up|last=Wood|first=Elliot|work=Formula Scout|date=2 April 2019|access-date=2 April 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.autosport.com/f3/news/142280/formula-european-masters-series-cancelled|title=DTM-supporting Formula European Masters series for F3 cars cancelled|last=Simmons|first=Marcus|work=[[Autosport]]|publisher=[[Motorsport Network]]|date=22 March 2019|access-date=2 April 2019}}</ref> Lawson won the opening races at [[Circuit Paul Ricard|Paul Ricard]], and also in [[Pau Grand Prix|Pau]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Wood|first=Ida|date=27 April 2019|title=Liam Lawson wins first race of Euroformula Open season at Paul Ricard|url=https://formulascout.com/liam-lawson-wins-first-race-of-euroformula-open-season-at-paul-ricard/50028|access-date=25 May 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Wood|first=Ida|date=18 May 2019|title=Liam Lawson dominates first Euroformula Open race in Pau|url=https://formulascout.com/lawson-wins-pau-race-1/50632|access-date=25 May 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> He would go on to take two more victories to become runner-up to [[Marino Sato]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Wood|first=Ida|date=21 September 2019|title=Marino Sato seals EF Open title at Barcelona as Liam Lawson wins|url=https://formulascout.com/liam-lawson-wins-race-marino-sato-seals-ef-open-title-barcelona/54690|access-date=25 May 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> He did however, win the rookies' championship.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Wood|first=Ida|date=13 October 2019|title=Liam Lawson seals EF Open rookie title in historic race at Monza|url=https://formulascout.com/lawson-wins-historic-final-ef-open-race/55459|access-date=25 May 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref>

=== International Formula 3 ===
In November 2018, Lawson competed in the season finale of the [[2018 F3 Asian Championship|Asian F3 championship]] with Irish outfit Pinnacle Motorsport.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.formulascout.com/eurocup-star-yifei-ye-to-make-f3-debut-at-sepang-this-weekend/46548|title=Yifei Ye and Liam Lawson to make F3 debuts at Sepang this weekend|last=Wood|first=Elliot|work=Formula Scout|date=21 November 2018|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref> He proceeded to dominate the weekend, taking all wins, fastest laps and pole positions on offer to finish eighth in the championship.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.formulascout.com/adac-f4-star-liam-lawson-stuns-on-asian-f3-debut-at-sepang/46613|title=ADAC F4 star Liam Lawson stuns on Asian F3 debut at Sepang|last=Wood|first=Elliot|work=Formula Scout|date=24 November 2018|access-date=25 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.formulascout.com/hyman-wins-asian-f3-title-as-lawson-completes-triple-win/46667|title=Hyman wins Asian F3 title as Lawson completes triple win|last=Wood|first=Elliot|work=Formula Scout|date=25 November 2018|access-date=25 November 2018}}</ref>

=== FIA Formula 3 Championship ===
==== 2019 ====
[[File:FIA F3 Austria 2019 Nr. 4 Lawson 1.jpg|thumb|Lawson racing for [[MP Motorsport]] during the [[2019 Spielberg Formula 3 round]]]]
In March 2019, Lawson joined [[MP Motorsport]] to contest the [[2019 FIA Formula 3 Championship|inaugural FIA Formula 3 Championship]], alongside [[Richard Verschoor]] and [[Simo Laaksonen]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Liam Lawson joins MP Motorsport for debut FIA F3 campaign|date=10 March 2019|url=https://www.fiaformula3.com/Latest/2HtLbQj21p7F0brmuqVFo4/liam-lawson-joins-mp-motorsport-for-debut-fia-f3-campaign|access-date=25 May 2022|website=FIA_Formula 3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}</ref> His debut at [[2019 Barcelona Formula 3 round|Barcelona]] proved challenging, qualifying lowest of the MP Motorsport drivers and retired from Race 1 due to a throttle motor failure.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/throttle-motor-failure-handicaps-lawson-in-opening-race/|title=Throttle motor failure handicaps Lawson in opening race of FIA F3 at Barcelona|date=12 May 2019|access-date=25 May 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=28 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220528044009/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/throttle-motor-failure-handicaps-lawson-in-opening-race/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Improvement followed in [[2019 Le Castellet Formula 3 round|Paul Ricard]], where he scored his first points with ninth place in Race 1 and further advanced to fifth in Race 2.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/two-points-scoring-races-for-lawson/|title=Two points scoring races for Lawson at the French Grand Prix with penalty overturned|date=24 June 2019|access-date=25 May 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=25 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220525143026/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/two-points-scoring-races-for-lawson/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Waring|first=Bethonie|date=24 June 2019|title=Liam Lawson given fifth place after FIA F3 penalty reversed|url=https://formulascout.com/liam-lawson-given-fifth-place-after-fia-f3-penalty-reversed/51638|access-date=25 May 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> After failing to score in [[2019 Spielberg Formula 3 round|Austria]], he rebounded in [[2019 Silverstone Formula 3 round|Silverstone]] and held the lead for half of the race before being overtaken by [[Leonardo Pulcini]] and [[Robert Shwartzman]], eventually securing third place.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson praises "Big changes" following first F3 podium|date=16 July 2019|url=https://www.fiaformula3.com/Latest/7oUgrZgqz66h8NZMEKedZ8/lawson-praises-big-changes-following-first-f3-podium|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_Formula 3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}</ref> The result marked both Lawson's and MP Motorsport's first podium of the season.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/first-podium-for-lawson/|title=First podium for Lawson|date=15 July 2019|access-date=25 May 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=25 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220525143028/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/first-podium-for-lawson/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Lawson endured scoreless rounds in [[2019 Budapest Formula 3 round|Hungary]] and [[2019 Spa-Francorchamps Formula 3 round|Spa-Francorchamps]] before returning to form in [[2019 Monza Formula 3 round|Monza]]. After qualifying thirteenth, he progressed to seventh in Race 1, giving him a front row start in Race 2. Although he dropped positions at the start, Lawson recovered and passed [[Fabio Scherer]] and [[Jake Hughes]] to finish second behind [[Yuki Tsunoda]], earning his second podium.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Terrific Tsunoda soars to first F3 win in Monza|date=8 September 2019|url=https://www.fiaformula3.com/Latest/2MYle79hI3wSotwFflC9LR/terrific-tsunoda-soars-to-first-f3-win-in-monza|access-date=26 May 2022|website=FIA_Formula 3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=2019 Round 7 post-Race 2 Quotes|date=8 September 2019|url=https://www.fiaformula3.com/Latest/7lC1QcgrtVzLz8OoN4hnBL/2019-round-7-post-race-2-quotes|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_Formula 3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}</ref> In the final round in [[2019 Sochi Formula 3 round|Sochi]], Lawson made up ten positions in Race 2 to finish eighth, after overtaking [[Max Fewtrell]] and [[Christian Lundgaard]] on the last lap.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-saves-his-best-for-last/|title=Lawson saves his best for last|date=1 October 2019|access-date=26 May 2022|website=liamlawson30.com}}{{Dead link|date=April 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> Overall, Lawson concluded the season eleventh in the championship with 41 points, scoring both of the team's podium finishes.

===== Macau Grand Prix =====
Just two weeks after the final round, Lawson was announced to compete at that year's [[2019 Macau Grand Prix|Macau Grand Prix]], remaining with MP Motorsport.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-confirmed-for-macau-grand-prix/|title=Lawson Confirmed for Macau Grand Prix|date=17 October 2019|access-date=26 May 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=26 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526093334/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-confirmed-for-macau-grand-prix/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Lawson qualified fifteenth, but in the qualification race slipped back to twentieth, before eventually coming through to finish seventh.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://motorsport.org.nz/lawson-storms-through-the-field-at-macau-gp/|title=Lawson Storms Through the Field at Macau GP|date=18 November 2019|access-date=26 May 2022|website=motorsport.org.nz}}</ref>

==== 2020 ====
Lawson moved to [[Hitech Grand Prix]] for the {{FIAF3|2020}} season, partnering [[Max Fewtrell]] and [[Red Bull Junior Team|Red Bull junior]] [[Dennis Hauger]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson and Hauger complete Hitech GP line-up|date=28 January 2020|url=https://www.fiaformula3.com/Latest/XOqF9VSw0MNZh1oW7HaKh/lawson-and-hauger-complete-hitech-gp-line-up|access-date=26 May 2022|website=FIAFormula3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}</ref> He opened the season in [[2020 Spielberg Formula 3 round|Austria]] by qualifying twelfth and improving to sixth in the first race. and charged to sixth. In Race 2, Lawson climbed to third before overtaking [[Clément Novalak]] and [[David Beckmann]] to move into the race lead. He resisted late-race pressure from former teammate [[Richard Verschoor]] to secure his maiden Formula 3 victory.<ref>{{cite news |author1=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |title=WORLD WRAP: Piastri leads F3 Championship |url=https://speedcafe.com/world-wrap-piastri-leads-f3-championship/ |access-date=6 July 2020 |work=Speed Cafe |date=6 July 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Pressure "not too much of an issue," for Race 2 winner Lawson|date=6 July 2020|url=https://www.fiaformula3.com/Latest/5A4nii9A7nxdd3cu9CaPpP/pressure-not-too-much-of-an-issue-for-race-2-winner-lawson|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_Formula 3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}</ref> During the [[2020 2nd Spielberg Formula 3 round|second Austrian round]], Lawson qualified tenth and finished eighth place in wet conditions in Race 1. Starting third in Race 2, he moved into the lead after passing [[Jake Hughes]] and [[Théo Pourchaire]]. A prolonged battle ultimately ended in a collision between the pair on lap 21, resulting in a double retirement which handed the win to Pourchaire.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Pourchaire propelled to first F3 win after late collision between Hughes and Lawson|date=12 July 2020|url=https://www.fiaformula3.com/Latest/2hlhbOkaQBFkpmIhwebHKY/pourchaire-propelled-to-first-f3-win-after-late-collision-between-hughes-and|access-date=26 May 2022|website=FIAFormula3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}</ref> Lawson endured a difficult weekend in [[2020 Budapest Formula 3 round|Hungary]], retiring from both races due to mechanical failures.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Two engine failures sidline Lawson in Hungary|date=20 July 2020|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/two-engine-failures-sideline-lawson-in-hungary/|access-date=26 May 2022|website=FIA_Formula3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}{{Dead link|date=July 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

From [[2020 Silverstone Formula 3 round|Silverstone]] onwards, Lawson scored points in every remaining race of the season. There, he narrowly missed out on pole position,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Wood|first=Ida|date=31 July 2020|title=Lawson: Hitech GP's work 'paid off' with Silverstone front row|url=https://formulascout.com/lawson-hitech-gps-work-paid-off-with-silverstone-front-row/65406|access-date=12 August 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> but overtook polesitter [[Logan Sargeant]] on the opening lap to claim his second win of the year.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/fia-f3/news/silverstone-report-lawson-piastri-sargeant/4847392/?filters%5Bdriver%5D%5B0%5D%5Btitle%5D=Liam+Lawson&filters%5Bdriver%5D%5B0%5D%5Bvalue%5D=451769|date=1 August 2020|title=Silverstone F3: Lawson fends off Piastri for victory|access-date=26 May 2022|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Woollard|first=Craig|date=1 August 2020|title=Lawson: Race wasn't 'under complete control' in Silverstone win|url=https://formulascout.com/lawson-race-wasnt-under-complete-control-in-silverstone-win/65473|access-date=12 August 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> He added a seventh place in Race 2. During the second [[2020 2nd Silverstone Formula 2 round|Silverstone round]], Lawson again qualified second<ref>{{Cite web|title=2020 Round 5 post-Qualifying press conference|date=7 August 2020|url=https://www.fiaformula3.com/Latest/1IDGxtdlmXmxtf7kV2ayRN/2020-round-5-post-qualifying-press-conference|access-date=2 January 2026|website=FIA_Formula 3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}</ref> and finished third in the opening race,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Woollard|first=Craig|date=8 August 2020|title=Lawson's pace 'not dissimilar' to Silverstone win despite third place|url=https://formulascout.com/lawsons-pace-not-dissimilar-to-silverstone-win-despite-third-place/65972|access-date=26 May 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> before narrowly missing a podium in Race 2 after running wide while battling Pourchaire.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Another F3 podium for Lawson|date=10 August 2020|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/another-f3-podium-for-lawson/|access-date=14 August 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=14 August 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220814025436/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/another-f3-podium-for-lawson/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In [[2020 Barcelona Formula 3 round|Barcelona]], Lawson qualified third despite an engine issue compromising his pole position attempt.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Woollard|first=Craig|date=14 August 2020|title=Lawson believes engine issues cost him shot at Barcelona F3 pole|url=https://formulascout.com/lawson-believes-engine-issues-cost-him-shot-at-barcelona-f3-pole/66321|access-date=26 May 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> He finished second in Race 1 after overtaking Sargeant late on;<ref>{{Cite web|title=2020 Round 6 post-Race 1 press conference|date=15 August 2020|url=https://www.fiaformula3.com/Latest/5IB4lIwvkXdRbHXXHJYz8u/2020-round-6-post-race-1-press-conference|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_Formula 3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}</ref> followed by seventh in Race 2, which elevated him to third in the championship standings.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Another podium puts Lawson to third|date=17 August 2020|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/another-podium-puts-lawson-to-third/|access-date=14 August 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=28 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220528051949/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/another-podium-puts-lawson-to-third/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In [[2020 Spa-Francorchamps Formula 3 round|Spa-Francorchamps]], Lawson finished seventh in Race 1 and recovered to third in Race 2 after losing positions at the start.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2020 Round 7 Race 2 Quotes|date=30 August 2020|url=https://www.fiaformula3.com/Latest/7tcXUxCUVVAgJzcarhk2dn/2020-round-7-race-2-quotes|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_Formula 3® - The Official F3® Website|language=en}}</ref> Lawson initially qualified fourth in [[2020 Monza Formula 3 round|Monza]]. Lawson inherited pole position in Monza following post-qualifying penalties.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Woollard|first=Craig|date=4 September 2020|title=Lawson promoted to FIA F3 pole after Monza qualifying penalties|url=https://formulascout.com/lawson-promoted-to-fia-f3-pole-after-monza-qualifying-penalties/67636|access-date=26 May 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> Damage sustained in early contact with [[Matteo Nannini]] limited him to sixth in Race 1. In Race 2, he finished second on the road after briefly leading, but a post-race penalty for forcing another driver off-track dropped him to seventh.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/post-race-penalty-robs-lawson-of-monza-podium/|title=Post-race Penalty Robs Lawson of Monza Podium|date=7 September 2020|access-date=26 May 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=26 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526100340/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/post-race-penalty-robs-lawson-of-monza-podium/|url-status=dead}}</ref> He concluded his season in [[2020 Mugello Formula 3 round|Mugello]], by finishing tenth in the first race, before dominating Race 2 to secure his third victory of the season.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Suttil|first=Josh|url=https://www.motorsport.com/fia-f3/news/mugello-f3-piastri-crowned-champion-lawson-wins-final-race/4874145/?filters%5Bdriver%5D%5B0%5D%5Btitle%5D=Liam+Lawson&filters%5Bdriver%5D%5B0%5D%5Bvalue%5D=451769|date=13 September 2020|title=Mugello F3: Piastri crowned champion as Lawson wins final race|access-date=26 May 2022|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> Lawson finished fifth in the championship with 143 points, recording three wins and six podiums throughout the season.

=== Road to Indy ===
In December 2017, Lawson partook in the [[2017 Mazda Road to Indy Shootout|Mazda Road to Indy Shootout]], finishing as the fastest driver but losing out on the scholarship to Ireland's [[Keith Donegan]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.usf2000.com/news/mazda-road-to-indy-shoot-out-next-for-lawson|title=Mazda Road to Indy Shoot-out next for Lawson|date=15 November 2017|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.formulascout.com/new-zealand-formula-ford-star-liam-lawson-joins-var-for-adac-f4/41451|title=New Zealand star Liam Lawson joins VAR for ADAC F4|last=Wood|first=Elliot|work=FormulaScout|date=15 March 2018|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://ff1600website.com/2017/12/11/keith-donegan-wins-mazda-road-to-indy-shootout/|title=Keith Donegan wins Mazda Road to Indy Shootout|date=11 December 2017|access-date=24 November 2018}}</ref>

=== FIA Formula 2 Championship ===
==== 2021 ====
In January 2021, Lawson was announced to be competing in the [[2021 FIA Formula 2 Championship|FIA Formula 2 Championship]] with [[Hitech Grand Prix]] alongside [[Jüri Vips]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Hitech sign Red Bull juniors Lawson and Vips for 2021|date=15 January 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/5pjviVWpHgte3vsrHwd77n/hitech-sign-red-bull-juniors-lawson-and-vips-for-2021|access-date=15 May 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> Lawson qualified eighth on his debut in [[2021 Sakhir Formula 2 round|Bahrain]]. In the opening sprint race, he made a strong start to take the lead and successfully defended against late pressure from [[Jehan Daruvala]] to secure his maiden win on debut.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Wood|first=Ida|title=Red Bull junior Lawson wins on F2 debut in Bahrain · RaceFans|date=27 March 2021|url=https://www.racefans.net/2021/03/27/red-bull-junior-lawson-wins-on-f2-debut-in-bahrain/|access-date=27 March 2021|website=RaceFans|language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Woollard|first=Craig|date=27 March 2021|title=Lawson: 'I thought Daruvala was going to get me' before debut F2 win|url=https://formulascout.com/lawson-i-thought-daruvala-was-going-to-get-me-before-debut-f2-win/76816|access-date=15 May 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> His fortunes were reversed in the second sprint however, as Lawson was taken out by [[Felipe Drugovich]] in a three-way scrap for third place.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/sensational-start-for-lawson-in-f2/|title=Sensational start for Lawson in F2|date=29 March 2021|access-date=22 May 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=28 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220528040732/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/sensational-start-for-lawson-in-f2/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Nevertheless, he redeemed himself with third place in the feature after a pass on [[Richard Verschoor]] on the last lap.<ref>{{Cite web|title=FEATURE RACE: Zhou fights to first Feature Race win with inspired performance in Sakhir|date=28 March 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/2KxPFaIoNmlPKIuqbLYL0l/feature-race-zhou-fights-to-first-feature-race-win-with-inspired-performance|access-date=22 May 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson's Guest Column: We didn't expect to win so early on|date=1 April 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/39EnU5NGJPMFvhzsD0HaXQ/lawsons-guest-column-we-didnt-expect-to-win-so-early-on|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> Lawson ended the opening round second in the championship. In [[2021 Monte Carlo Formula 2 round|Monaco]], Lawson qualified twelfth and finished ninth in the first sprint race. He started the second sprint from pole position after [[Marcus Armstrong]] was unable to take the grid. After briefly losing the lead, Lawson reclaimed first place with an overtake on [[Oscar Piastri]] at  ''Rascasse'' and went on to take the chequered flag in first.<ref>{{Cite web|title=SPRINT RACE 2: Lawson recovers from a slow start to earn brilliant wet win in Monte Carlo|date=22 May 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/4hvjPMYGEFKdqUuWDHmyVm/sprint-race-2-lawson-recovers-from-a-slow-start-to-win-in-the-wet-at|access-date=22 May 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> However, he was later disqualified for using a prohibited throttle map, handing victory to [[Dan Ticktum]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Lawson stripped of Monaco F2 sprint win, Ticktum inherits victory|url=https://www.autosport.com/formula2/news/lawson-stripped-of-monaco-f2-sprint-win-ticktum-inherits-victory/6513188/|access-date=26 May 2021|website=autosport.com|language=en}}</ref> He later described his disqualification as "hard to swallow".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Howard|first=Tom|url=https://www.motorsport.com/fia-f2/news/lawson-reaction-losing-monaco-win-throttle-map/6514066/|date=23 May 2023|title=Lawson: Losing Monaco F2 win "a tough one to swallow"|access-date=16 July 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> He finished seventh in the feature race.<ref>{{Cite web|title='At least I got to make a statement' – Lawson reflects on getting back in the car after Sprint Race 2 disqualification|date=25 May 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/1WGeIQfWmrdFoz6WQ8zvp4/at-least-i-got-to-make-a-statement-lawson-reflects-on-getting-back-in-the|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref>

Lawson claimed his maiden pole position in [[2021 Baku Formula 2 round|Baku]], forming a front row for Hitech.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Waring|first=Bethonie|date=4 June 2021|title=Lawson takes first F2 pole in Hitech GP one-two in Baku|url=https://formulascout.com/lawson-takes-first-f2-pole-in-hitech-gp-one-two-in-baku/79578|access-date=22 May 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Woollard|first=Craig|date=4 June 2021|title=F2 poleman Lawson thanks Vips for gains made before qualifying|url=https://formulascout.com/f2-poleman-lawson-thanks-vips-for-gains-made-before-qualifying/79594|access-date=12 August 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> His opening sprint race ended early after contact with Piastri broke his suspension,<ref>{{Cite web|title=SPRINT RACE 1: Shwartzman surges to a dominant first win of the season in Baku|date=5 June 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/4NiMkLaZghwA4qzVKGkzjr/sprint-race-1-shwartzman-surges-to-a-dominant-first-win-of-the-season-in|access-date=16 July 2023|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> but recovered to finish seventh in the second sprint despite a power loss.<ref>{{Cite web|title='If it was me, it's a silly mistake, but at least the pace was there' – Lawson believes loss of power cost him a podium|date=5 June 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/4GnzsU9DC7L55eIBAWNMV9/if-it-was-me-its-a-silly-mistake-but-at-least-the-pace-was-there-lawson|access-date=22 May 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> In the feature race, an aggressive defence on [[Théo Pourchaire]] during the opening lap earned Lawson a ten-second penalty; he eventually finished sixth.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson 'Disappointed' With Messy Baku Weekend: 'It's Not At All What We Wanted'|date=8 June 2021|url=https://feederseries.net/2021/06/08/lawson-rues-messy-baku-weekend-its-not-at-all-what-we-wanted/|access-date=2 January 2026|website=Feeder Series|language=en}}</ref> In [[2021 Silverstone Formula 2 round|Silverstone]], Lawson qualified eleventh and scored points in both sprint races, finishing seventh and fifth respectively. He placed just outside the points in the feature race.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Vips podiums in Silverstone F2|date=18 July 2021|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/juniorteam/vips-podiums-in-silverstone-f2|access-date=16 July 2023|publisher=[[Red Bull]]|website=redbull.com|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=My mid-season report card: Liam Lawson|date=13 August 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/Sy8iBMCbRlVL2ek5H4IHG/my-mid-season-report-card-liam-lawson|access-date=14 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> In [[2021 Monza Formula 2 round|Monza]], Lawson qualified fourth but front wing damage forced him into a pit stop early during the opening sprint race; he managed to recover to fifth.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson focused on the positives but admits he was 'disappointed' by Turn 1 collision|date=11 September 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/2gE5wROZCTbMXI81nXN3Nh/lawson-focused-on-the-positives-but-admits-he-was-disappointed-by-turn-1|access-date=22 May 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> He finished fourth in the second sprint and ran as high as second in the feature race before retiring due to a power issue.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawsons-f2-charge-literally-extinguished/|title=Lawson's F2 charge literally extinguished in bizarre late incident at Monza, Italy|date=13 September 2021|access-date=22 May 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=22 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220522080151/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawsons-f2-charge-literally-extinguished/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Lawson qualified eighth in [[2021 Sochi Formula 2 round|Sochi]],<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson disappointed with Qualifying result but knows races mean 'anything can happen'|date=24 September 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/60bvHo7df5LyFgOQKjxrCu/lawson-disappointed-with-qualifying-result-but-knows-races-mean-anything-can|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> but retired from the first sprint after damaging his suspension while running third.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Ticktum opens up on future: 'Anyone with half a brain will know it's IndyCar, DTM or F2'|date=26 September 2021|url=https://feederseries.net/2021/09/26/ticktum-opens-up-on-future-anyone-with-half-a-brain-will-know-its-indycar-dtm-or-f2/|access-date=2 January 2026|website=Feeder Series|language=en}}</ref> Lawson finished seventh in the feature race after a slow start.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Daruvala and Vips podium in Sochi F2|date=26 September 2021|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/juniorteam/daruvala-and-vips-podium-in-sochi-f2|access-date=2 January 2026|website=[[Red Bull]]|language=en}}</ref>

In [[2021 Jeddah Formula 2 round|Jeddah]], Lawson qualified tenth and started from reverse pole in the first sprint, but was outdragged by [[Marcus Armstrong]] on the opening lap; nevertheless he finished second which marked his first podium since Bahrain.<ref>{{Cite web|title=SPRINT RACE 1: Armstrong wrestles to maiden victory over fellow Kiwi Lawson in first race at Jeddah|date=4 December 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/2Fz0Tyh0jyh2Fid7J6HBMp/sprint-race-1-armstrong-wrestles-to-maiden-victory-over-fellow-kiwi-lawson|access-date=23 May 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=2021 Round 7 post-Sprint Race 1 press conference|date=4 December 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/5uotiB4hCaOoJlQq3Coe0t/2021-round-7-post-sprint-race-1-press-conference|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> He retired from the second sprint after crashing out late in the race,<ref>{{Cite web|title=SPRINT RACE 2: Piastri closes in on the title with victory over Viscaal in Jeddah|date=4 December 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/5wRq6hIsWVJiz1uo3efW9N/sprint-race-2-piastri-closes-in-on-the-title-with-victory-over-viscaal-in|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> and finished ninth in an aborted feature race.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson says Jeddah not dangerous|date=7 December 2021|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-says-jeddah-not-dangerous/|access-date=14 August 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=14 August 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220814070135/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-says-jeddah-not-dangerous/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Lawson qualified seventh for the [[2021 Yas Island Formula 2 round|Yas Marina finale]]. He finished fifth and sixth in the sprint races,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson on the chaotic start to Sprint Race 2 and his chances in the final Feature Race of 2021|date=12 December 2021|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/7enzsPoUIJRZE2wSfAA2MX/lawson-on-the-chaotic-start-to-sprint-race-2-and-his-chances-in-the-final|access-date=12 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> but retired from the feature race due to an engine issue.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Jehan Daruvala wins F2 in Abu Dhabi|date=12 November 2021|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/juniorteam/jehan-daruvala-wins-f2-in-abu-dhabi|access-date=16 July 2023|publisher=[[Red Bull]]|website=redbull.com|language=en}}</ref> Lawson concluded his rookie season ninth in the championship with 103 points, scoring one win and three podiums.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Driver Standings for the FIA Formula 2 2021 Championship|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Standings/Driver?seasonId=178|access-date=16 July 2023|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref>

==== 2022 ====
[[File:FIA F2 Austria 2022 Nr. 5 Lawson.jpg|thumb|left|Lawson driving the [[Dallara F2 2018]] during the [[2022 Spielberg Formula 2 round]]]]
Lawson switched to [[Carlin Motorsport|Carlin]] for the [[2022 Formula 2 Championship|2022 season]] alongside American [[Logan Sargeant]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Lawson moves to Carlin for the 2022 campaign|date=14 January 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/5O2bddhXmE8wwPAtiPTUmU/lawson-moves-to-carlin-for-the-2022-campaign|access-date=14 January 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Logan Sargeant joins Carlin for 2022 FIA Formula 2 Championship // Infinity Sports Management|date=13 December 2021|url=https://www.infinitysportsmanagement.co.uk/press/logan-sargeant-joins-carlin-for-2022-fia-formula-2-championship|access-date=27 April 2022|website=infinitysportsmanagement.co.uk|language=en}}{{Dead link|date=April 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

He began his campaign strongly in [[2022 Sakhir Formula 2 round|Bahrain]], qualifying sixth. He finished third in the sprint after passing [[Ralph Boschung]] late,<ref>{{Cite web|title=2022 Round 1 post-Sprint Race press conference|date=19 March 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/rs0LzFEt6oeK0rATNlzP4/2022-round-1-post-sprint-race-press-conference-f2|access-date=14 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> and followed this with second place in the feature race after avoiding incidents among the leading runners, thereby coming away with a double podium.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2022 Round 1 post-Feature Race press conference|date=20 March 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/34b0xYtAGujEePw6fdm8gw/2022-round-1-post-feature-race-press-conference|access-date=3 January 2026|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> Following the round, Lawson noted that he felt more comfortable in Formula 2 after contesting a dual campaign in the [[2021 Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters|2021 DTM season]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=White|first=Megan|url=https://www.motorsport.com/fia-f2/news/lawson-comfortable-focusing-f2-dual-2021-campaign/9253401/?filters%5Bdriver%5D%5B0%5D%5Btitle%5D=Liam+Lawson&filters%5Bdriver%5D%5B0%5D%5Bvalue%5D=451769|date=24 March 2022|title=Lawson "more comfortable" focusing on F2 after dual 2021 campaign|access-date=14 August 2022|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> In [[2022 Jeddah Formula 2 round|Jeddah]], Lawson qualified fifth and soon found himself in podium contention during the sprint race after [[Dennis Hauger]]'s pit lane error. Following a safety car restart, he swiftly overcame [[Calan Williams]] and [[Jake Hughes]] to secure his first win of the season.<ref>{{Cite web|last=White|first=Megan|url=https://www.motorsport.com/fia-f2/news/saudi-arabia-lawson-wins-sprint-race/9339043/?filters%5Bdriver%5D%5B0%5D%5Btitle%5D=Liam+Lawson&filters%5Bdriver%5D%5B0%5D%5Bvalue%5D=451769|date=26 March 2022|title=F2 Saudi Arabia: Lawson wins ahead of Vips in chaotic sprint race|access-date=27 March 2022|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> He was set to continue his podium streak running third during the feature race, but retired due to a loose wheel immediately after his pit stop,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/wheel-falls-off-lawsons-podium-streak/|title=Wheel falls off Lawson's podium streak|date=28 March 2022|access-date=27 March 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=28 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220528052013/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/wheel-falls-off-lawsons-podium-streak/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson's Guest Column: I was in a good headspace with the way that I was driving|date=1 April 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/2iHxwylQShKHw0KLyIo2Nx/lawsons-guest-column-i-was-in-a-good-headspace-with-the-way-that-i-was|access-date=14 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> nevertheless he retained second place in the championship.

Lawson endured a difficult triple-header thereafter. In [[2022 Imola Formula 2 round|Imola]], he qualified fourteenth and recovered to eighth in the sprint race,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson 'made the most' of Sprint Race opportunities following qualifying disappointment|date=23 April 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/10lDVSDgDxCgNudpcKRDwj/lawson-made-the-most-of-sprint-race-opportunities-following-qualifying|access-date=14 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> but retired from the feature race after a steering wheel failure caused a crash.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/broken-steering-sends-lawsons-car-into-the-wall/|title=Broken steering sends Lawson's car into the wall|date=25 April 2022|access-date=27 May 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=27 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220527100354/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/broken-steering-sends-lawsons-car-into-the-wall/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In [[2022 Barcelona Formula 2 round|Barcelona]], Lawson qualified sixteenth and narrowly missed points in the sprint race.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson holds onto fourth after a difficult weekend in Spain|date=23 May 2022|url=https://www.liamlawson30.com/news/lawson-holds-onto-fourth-after-a-difficult-weekend-in-spain/|access-date=3 January 2026|website=liamlawson30.com|language=en|archive-date=6 January 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260106161743/https://www.liamlawson30.com/news/lawson-holds-onto-fourth-after-a-difficult-weekend-in-spain/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Despite a strong start in the feature race, he faded to ninth after losing places to cars on fresher tyres.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson playing to his strengths with Barcelona Feature Race fightback|date=23 May 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/5XTcYoTWRPlJhYEPqNCAgf/lawson-playing-to-his-strengths-with-barcelona-feature-race-fightback|access-date=27 May 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> In [[2022 Monte Carlo Formula 2 round|Monaco]], Lawson initially secured pole position,<ref>{{Cite web|title=QUALIFYING: Lawson leaves it late to set pole in Monte Carlo|date=27 May 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/3CCKmC1whs5tfr5Ue2qY2V/qualifying-lawson-leaves-it-late-to-set-pole-in-monte-carlo|access-date=14 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> but his lap was deleted for failing to slow under yellow flags, and he received a five-place grid penalty for the sprint race.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Monte Carlo post-Qualifying penalties|date=27 May 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/6upQwWAfGw2OluhanEluqP/monte-carlo-post-qualifying-penalties|access-date=14 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> He scored a point with eighth in the sprint but stalled on the grid in the feature race and later retired with engine issues.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Disastrous weekend for Lawson|date=30 May 2022|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/disastrous-weekend-for-lawson/|access-date=14 August 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=14 August 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220814070135/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/disastrous-weekend-for-lawson/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Lawson returned to form in [[2022 Baku Formula 2 round|Baku]], qualifying second.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2022 Round 6 post-Qualifying press conference|date=10 June 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/4lhCvPEeomSaIH15DHsmdy/2022-round-6-post-qualifying-press-conference|access-date=14 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> He finished third after a strong restart late in the race saw him gain multiple positions,<ref>{{Cite web|title=2022 Round 6 post-Sprint Race press conference|date=11 June 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/5WHsYmCqwsNgmkKQg0qB9j/2022-round-6-post-sprint-race-press-conference|access-date=14 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> but suffered a puncture after being hit by [[Jack Doohan]] in the feature race; he was forced to pit which resulted in a fifteenth-place finish.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Another podium and another disappointment|date=13 June 2022|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/another-podium-and-another-disappointment/|access-date=14 August 2022|website=liamlawson30.com|archive-date=14 August 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220814070137/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/another-podium-and-another-disappointment/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

[[File:EU5A1169 (52616351785).jpg|thumb|Lawson secured four wins and ten podiums on his way to finishing third in the championship.]]
In [[2022 Silverstone Formula 2 round|Silverstone]], Lawson qualified fifth. Contact in the sprint race caused front wing damage which necessitated a pit stop, and he finished 20th. In the feature race, he made a strong start to move into third, and held the position to take another podium.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2022 Round 7 post-Feature Race press conference|date=3 July 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/5ii6gdQHCfTdLQiitgst4r/2022-round-7-post-feature-race-press-conference|access-date=3 January 2026|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> In the [[2022 Spielberg Formula 2 round|Austria]], Lawson qualified fourteenth and retired from the sprint race after stalling on the grid and encountering technical issues.<ref>{{Cite web|title=SPRINT RACE: Unassailable Armstrong reigns supreme with lights-to-flag display|date=9 July 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/4GwtEP1LjsOIBofPiO0mi7/sprint-race-unassailable-armstrong-reigns-supreme-with-lights-to-flag|access-date=16 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> He salvaged a point with tenth in the feature race after opting for slick tyres at the start.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Daruvala robbed of second in Spielberg F2|date=10 July 2022|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/juniorteam/daruvala-robbed-of-second-in-spielberg-f2-|access-date=16 July 2023|publisher=[[Red Bull]]|website=redbull.com|language=en}}</ref> Lawson qualified ninth in [[2022 Le Castellet Formula 2 round|Paul Ricard]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson hoping luck is on his side to capitalise on Carlin potential|date=23 July 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/377fPG77RGSzswioeIBWrg/lawson-hoping-luck-is-on-his-side-to-capitalise-on-carlin-potential|access-date=16 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> After briefly losing second at the start of the sprint race, Lawson eventually made the move for the lead with a late pass on [[Jehan Daruvala]], earning him his second win of the year.<ref>{{Cite web|title=SPRINT RACE: Lawson surges to victory in Le Castellet|date=23 July 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/6jAzO6xdzmGQipZnSKZpyy/sprint-race-lawson-surges-to-sprint-race-victory-at-le-castellet|access-date=16 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Alonso Lopez|first=Alejandro|date=23 July 2022|title=Lawson targets better start to reach top five in F2 feature race in France|url=https://formulascout.com/lawson-targets-better-start-to-reach-top-five-in-f2-feature-race-in-france/96456|access-date=16 August 2022|website=Formula Scout|language=en-US}}</ref> He finished sixth in the feature race.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Iwasa dominates Paul Ricard F2 Feature – Lawson & Daruvala 1-2 in Sprint|date=24 July 2022|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/juniorteam/iwasa-dominates-paul-ricard-f2-feature-lawson-daruvala-1-2-in-sprint|access-date=3 January 2026|website=[[Red Bull]]|language=en}}</ref> In [[2022 Budapest Formula 2 round|Hungary]], Lawson qualified eleventh and recovered to sixth in the sprint race.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Iwasa podiums from pole in Hungaroring F2|date=31 July 2022|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/juniorteam/iwasa-podiums-from-pole-in-hungaroring-f2|access-date=3 January 2026|website=[[Red Bull]]|language=en}}</ref> He placed seventh in the feature race on the alternate strategy.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson disappointed to miss out on more points but says Carlin speed is there|date=31 July 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/75ZM83vsrjaABGye5fHuPk/lawson-disappointed-to-miss-out-on-more-points-but-says-carlin-speed-is|access-date=16 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref>

At [[2022 Spa-Francorchamps Formula 2 round|Spa-Francorchamps]], Lawson qualified sixth. A good start moved him into second at the start, before overtaking [[Ralph Boschung]] on the next lap to secure another victory.<ref>{{Cite web|title=SPRINT RACE: Lawson in a league of his own in dramatic final lap showdown|date=27 August 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/1yfxXNnRVXAefyalG0BwKw/sprint-race-lawson-in-a-league-of-his-own-in-dramatic-final-lap-showdown|access-date=30 August 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Liam Lawson on his qualifying consistency in 2022: 'It's pretty horrendous'|date=28 August 2022|url=https://feederseries.net/2022/08/28/liam-lawson-on-his-qualifying-consistency-in-2022-its-pretty-horrendous/|access-date=3 January 2026|website=Feeder Series|language=en}}</ref> He followed this with a third place in the feature race after a battle with [[Enzo Fittipaldi]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=2022 Round 11 post-Feature Race press conference|date=28 August 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/SIpv1dsKLvfndRorn95xW/2022-round-11-post-feature-race-press-conference|access-date=3 January 2026|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> In [[2022 Zandvoort Formula 2 round|Zandvoort]], Lawson qualified sixth position and finished fourth in the sprint race after overtaking Vips at the start.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson extracted the maximum after toing and froing Sprint Race|date=4 September 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/3u2XJLofeKURuIexY961QI/lawson-extracted-the-maximum-after-toing-and-froing-sprint-race|access-date=6 September 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> A strategy gamble on hard tyres in the feature race was compromised by a safety car, leaving him twelfth at the finish.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Hauger and Iwasa podium in Zandvoort F2|date=5 September 2022|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/juniorteam/hauger-and-iwasa-podium-in-zandvoort-f2-2022|access-date=16 July 2023|publisher=[[Red Bull]]|website=redbull.com|language=en}}</ref> In [[2022 Monza Formula 2 round|Monza]], Lawson qualified second.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2022 Round 13 post-Qualifying press conference|date=9 September 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/6j5oD602KGRrCNeZWYsh4n/2022-round-13-post-qualifying-press-conference|access-date=16 September 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> He recovered from an average start in the sprint race to finish sixth,<ref>{{Cite web|title=SPRINT RACE: Vips breezes to victory as Drugovich claims 2022 F2 title|date=10 September 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/4DYTu8xCSKUQGPlquFzw83/sprint-race-vips-breezes-to-victory-as-drugovich-claims-2022-f2-title|access-date=16 September 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> later promoted to fifth following a post-race penalty for [[Richard Verschoor]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Verschoor and Boschung penalised following Monza Sprint Race|date=10 September 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/4fYvrveTnb6jAxrpGi6m5O/verschoor-and-boschung-penalised-following-monza-sprint-race|access-date=16 September 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> In the feature race, Lawson briefly led after overtaking Doohan at the start but lost time after failing to capitalise on a pit stop under the safety car. He was later spun around by Vips which caused front wing damage, and eventually finished thirteenth.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Double F2 podium for Monza Feature winner Daruvala|date=11 September 2022|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/juniorteam/double-f2-podium-for-monza-feature-winner-daruvala-2022|access-date=16 July 2023|publisher=[[Red Bull]]|website=redbull.com|language=en}}</ref> At the [[2022 Yas Island Formula 2 round|Abu Dhabi finale]], Lawson qualified ninth and secured his fourth victory of the season in the sprint race after overtaking Verschoor.<ref>{{Cite web|title=SPRINT RACE: Lawson breezes to fourth victory of 2022 campaign in Yas Marina|date=19 November 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/OBUQi6oHNC4wKe3E1Qy5n/sprint-race-lawson-breezes-to-fourth-victory-of-2022-campaign-in-yas-marina|access-date=25 December 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Wood|first=Ida|date=19 November 2022|title=Lawson “surprised” how lead grew in ultra-consistent run to F2 win|url=https://formulascout.com/lawson-surprised-how-lead-grew-in-ultra-consistent-run-to-f2-win/101102|access-date=3 January 2026|website=Formula Scout|language=en-GB}}</ref> In the feature race, he finished third after an early pit stop proved successful.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2022 Round 14 post-Feature Race press conference|date=20 November 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/1lTvzoZ7gdHCKVOKCVcTBe/2022-round-14-post-feature-race-press-conference|access-date=25 December 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref> Lawson concluded the season third in the drivers' championship with 149 points, four victories and ten podiums.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Driver Standings for the FIA Formula 2 2022 Championship|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Standings/Driver?seasonId=179|access-date=14 June 2022|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref>

== Formula One career ==
In February 2019, Lawson joined the [[Red Bull Junior Team]].<ref name="Red Bull">{{cite web|url=https://juniorteam.redbull.com/article/liam-lawson-joins-juniors-all-corners-global-challenge|title=Liam Lawson joins Juniors from all corners for global challenge|date=18 February 2019|access-date=18 February 2019}}</ref>

In July 2021, Lawson got his first experience in an F1 car at the 2021 [[Goodwood Festival of Speed]], driving the 2011 [[Red Bull RB7]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Liam Lawson to drive F1 car|date=8 July 2021|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/liam-lawson-to-drive-f1-car/|access-date=18 November 2022|website=liamlawson.co.nz|archive-date=18 November 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221118094415/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/liam-lawson-to-drive-f1-car/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Lawson took part in the Young Driver Test at the [[Yas Marina Circuit]] at the end of the [[2021 Formula One World Championship|2021 season]] with [[Scuderia AlphaTauri]], driving the [[AlphaTauri AT02|AT02]].<ref>{{Citation |title=Liam Lawson gets ready for the F1 Rookie Test | date=13 December 2021 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7bkVTkadoow |language=en |access-date=19 May 2022}}</ref> For the [[2022 Formula One World Championship|2022 season]], he served as a reserve/test driver for [[Scuderia AlphaTauri|AlphaTauri]]. In March 2022 [[Franz Tost]] revealed that Lawson would make his [[Formula One]] debut in a [[Formula One racing#Free practice|free practice session]] for them during the season.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2 March 2022 |title=Vips, Lawson set for FP1 debuts in 2022 |url=https://www.motorsportweek.com/2022/03/02/vips-lawson-set-for-fp1-debuts-in-2022/ |access-date=19 May 2022 |website=Motorsport Week |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Cooper |first=Adam |date=31 July 2022 |title=Lawson and Piastri lined up for F1 FP1 young driver runs |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/lawson-and-piastri-lined-up-for-f1-fp1-young-driver-runs/10345694/ |access-date=16 August 2022 |website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> He made his free practice debut at the [[2022 Belgian Grand Prix]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=AlphaTauri 2022 plan| url=https://twitter.com/alphataurif1/status/1561987637304508416 |access-date=23 August 2022 |website=Twitter |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson keen to take lessons and adjust back from 'epic' FP1 debut|date=27 August 2022|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/6yMj3aIGFAX0TS2IjI6JPu/lawson-keen-to-take-lessons-and-adjust-back-from-epic-fp1-debut|access-date=16 July 2023|website=FIA_F2® - The Official F2® Website|language=en}}</ref>

Following then-reserve driver [[Jüri Vips]]' exclamation of a racial slur during a [[Twitch (service)|Twitch]] livestream (in which, incidentally, Lawson was also present) during June 2022, team principal [[Christian Horner]] confirmed that Lawson had been promoted to replace Vips as the reserve driver for [[Red Bull Racing]], sharing this role with AlphaTauri.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Woodhouse |first=Jamie |date=2 July 2022 |title=Liam Lawson takes over from Juri Vips as Red Bull Formula 1 reserve driver |url=https://www.planetf1.com/news/liam-lawson-red-bull-reserve-driver/ |access-date=4 September 2022 |website=PlanetF1 |language=en}}</ref> Lawson made another FP1 appearance with AlphaTauri at the [[2022 Mexico City Grand Prix]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Red Bull-backed Liam Lawson to make another FP1 appearance with AlphaTauri at Mexico City GP|date=26 October 2022|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.red-bull-backed-liam-lawson-to-make-another-fp1-appearance-with-alphatauri.4BGCGHvs8xH5yMtNgkIOWU.html|access-date=26 October 2022|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=2022 Mexico City GP: Practice|date=28 October 2022|url=https://scuderia.alphatauri.com/en/2022-mexico-city-free-practice/|access-date=16 July 2023|website=[[Scuderia AlphaTauri]]|language=en}}</ref> He made his Red Bull debut during practice at the season-ending {{F1 GP|2022|Abu Dhabi}}.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Eight drivers set to sub in for FP1 in Abu Dhabi|date=17 November 2022|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.eight-drivers-set-to-sub-in-for-fp1-in-abu-dhabi.1N2Z5yEJ7zxo6SAe2tAxcq.html|access-date=18 November 2022|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> Lawson then took part in the post-season tests in Abu Dhabi driving the Red Bull.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Ferrari lead the way with 1-2-3 in busy post-season Abu Dhabi test {{!}} Formula 1®|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.ferrari-lead-the-way-in-busy-post-season-abu-dhabi-test.3xAKt5uxeATAAQF4gOALAY.html|access-date=24 November 2022|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref>

Lawson continued as reserve driver for Red Bull and AlphaTauri in [[2023 Formula One World Championship|2023]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Every F1 team's reserve driver for the 2023 season|date=20 February 2023|url=https://www.crash.net/f1/news/1018360/1/every-f1-teams-reserve-driver-2023-season|access-date=16 July 2023|website=crash.net|language=en}}</ref> In February, he drove the RB7 for a demonstration run during the [[2023 Bathurst 12 Hour]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Liam Lawson drives Red Bull Formula 1 at Bathurst!|date=9 February 2023|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/liam-lawson-drives-red-bull-formula-1-at-bathurst/|access-date=16 July 2023|website=liamlawson.co.nz|language=en|archive-date=16 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230716111830/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/liam-lawson-drives-red-bull-formula-1-at-bathurst/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Following the [[2023 British Grand Prix|British Grand Prix]], [[Nyck de Vries]] was removed from his seat at AlphaTauri, with [[Daniel Ricciardo]] being chosen ahead of Lawson to replace the Dutchman.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Ricciardo to replace De Vries at AlphaTauri from the Hungarian Grand Prix|date=11 July 2023|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-ricciardo-to-replace-de-vries-at-alphatauri-from-the-hungarian.2yhnzxE6XpSUZwzHfr9rXp.html|access-date=16 July 2023|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> Following the announcement, Lawson stated that he "understood their decision" and commented that jumping in mid-season would be "extremely tough".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/lawson-understands-why-alphatauri-chose-ricciardo-over-him-for-f1-seat/10495998/|date=15 July 2023|title=Lawson understands why AlphaTauri chose Ricciardo over him for F1 seat|access-date=16 July 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref>

=== AlphaTauri / Racing Bulls (2023–2024) ===
==== 2023: Debut in a reserve role ====
Lawson made his Formula One debut at the [[2023 Dutch Grand Prix]] for AlphaTauri, temporarily replacing Ricciardo after he sustained a broken hand in a crash in Friday's second free practice session.<ref name="CleerenAug2023">{{cite news |last1=Cleeren |first1=Filip |title=Ricciardo out of F1 Dutch GP with injured hand, Lawson to sub |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/ricciardo-out-of-f1-dutch-gp-with-broken-wrist/10511470/ |access-date=25 August 2023 |work=www.motorsport.com |date=25 August 2023 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Ricciardo to be replaced by Lawson for remainder of Dutch GP weekend after breaking hand|date=25 August 2023|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-ricciardo-to-be-replaced-by-lawson-for-remainder-of-dutch-gp.5JCEAa5XnnUsjZ1SE7lXFX.html|access-date=26 August 2023|website=formula1.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=25 August 2023 |title=F1's Ricciardo breaks hand, to miss Dutch GP |url=https://www.espn.com/f1/story/_/id/38257884/daniel-ricciardo-broken-hand-rookie-liam-lawson-steps-dutch-gp |access-date=28 August 2023 |website=ESPN.com |language=en}}</ref> With limited practice time which included a spin, he qualified in 20th.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson aiming for clean maiden Grand Prix after 'very challenging' debut qualifying in Zandvoort|date=27 August 2023|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.lawson-aiming-for-clean-maiden-grand-prix-after-very-challenging-debut.aGorFyfbrNb7JoQeDiyKP.html|access-date=29 August 2023|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> Despite a ten-second penalty for impeding [[Kevin Magnussen]] in the pits, Lawson finished in 13th place in a challenging and eventful debut race.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson admits he was 'just happy to get through the race' after chaotic maiden Grand Prix|date=28 August 2023|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.lawson-admits-he-was-just-happy-to-get-through-the-race-after-chaotic-maiden.hnpzpL2Vt9N1DXf9qz72x.html|access-date=29 August 2023|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> Most notably, it included battling with [[Scuderia Ferrari|Ferrari]]'s [[Charles Leclerc]] and finishing higher than his AlphaTauri teammate [[Yuki Tsunoda]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Woodhouse |first=Jamie |date=27 August 2023 |title=Liam Lawson reflects on 'frustrating' Charles Leclerc battle in 'eventful' F1 debut |url=https://www.planetf1.com/news/liam-lawson-charles-leclerc-battle-dutch-gp/ |access-date=28 August 2023 |website=PlanetF1 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Walsh |first=Fergal |date=27 August 2023 |title=Lawson reacts to 'sketchy' F1 debut |url=https://racingnews365.com/lawson-satisfied-after-sketchy-f1-debut |access-date=28 August 2023 |website=RacingNews365 |language=en}}</ref> On 28 August 2023, AlphaTauri team principal [[Franz Tost]] stated that Lawson would likely continue to race for the team at the [[2023 Italian Grand Prix]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=27 August 2023 |title=Ricciardo undergoes surgery and &quot;metal work&quot; on hand injury |url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/ricciardo-undergoes-surgery-and-metal-work-on-hand-injury/10512500/ |access-date=28 August 2023 |website=[[Autosport.com]] |language=en}}</ref> This statement was confirmed hours later, as Lawson would also be keeping his replacement role until Ricciardo is fit to race.<ref>{{Cite web|last=White|first=Megan|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/lawson-to-be-alphatauri-f1-replacement-until-ricciardo-fit/10512925/|date=28 August 2023|title=Lawson to be AlphaTauri F1 replacement until Ricciardo fit|access-date=29 August 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> Lawson qualified in 12th place at the [[2023 Italian Grand Prix]] and finished in 11th place.<ref>{{Cite web|title='11th is a bit disappointing' – Lawson frustrated to miss out on points in second Grand Prix appearance at Monza|date=7 September 2023|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.11th-is-a-bit-disappointing-lawson-frustrated-to-miss-out-on-points-in.4krciWjCI3XL92xXI3c95H.html|access-date=7 September 2023|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> At the [[2023 Singapore Grand Prix]], Lawson qualified a career-best tenth place, most notably, knocking championship leader [[Max Verstappen]] out of the second part of qualifying.<ref>{{Cite news|date=13 September 2023|title=Lawson keeps Ricciardo's drive for Singapore|url=https://www.espn.co.uk/f1/story/_/id/38391268/liam-lawson-continue-racing-daniel-ricciardo-place-singapore-gp |access-date=14 September 2023 |work=ESPN|language=en|last=Saunders|first=Nate}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Liam Lawson Qualifies P10 for Singapore GP|date=16 September 2023|url=https://honda.racing/f1/post/f1-2023-rd16-qualifying|access-date=23 September 2023|website=honda.racing|language=en}}</ref> After fending off [[Alex Albon]] during the last laps, Lawson finished the race in ninth, scoring points for the first time in Formula One as well as becoming the second AlphaTauri driver that year to score.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.lawson-hails-fantastic-first-f1-points-scoring-outing-after-finishing-ninth.285FTO9nMyxPKFYho4uWco.html|title=Lawson hails 'fantastic' first F1 points scoring outing after finishing ninth in Singapore|work=[[Formula 1]]|date=18 September 2023|access-date=19 September 2023}}</ref>

Lawson finished in eleventh at the [[2023 Japanese Grand Prix]], ahead of Tsunoda in 12th.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson took no 'satisfaction' from beating Tsunoda in Japan after missing out on 2024 AlphaTauri seat|date=26 September 2023|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.lawson-took-no-satisfaction-from-beating-tsunoda-in-japan-after-missing-out.5aVJF9mwEpVQPBadC62gDV.html|access-date=26 October 2023|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> Lawson endured a rough final appearance at the {{F1 GP|2023|Qatar}},<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson reveals how a 'random' FaceTime call from Ricciardo informed him he would be driving in Qatar|date=5 October 2023|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.lawson-reveals-how-a-random-facetime-call-from-ricciardo-informed-him-he.4p7qrsaliZrflwhg0A8L6b.html|access-date=26 October 2023|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> where he spun out of the sprint and finished last of the runners during the race.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Kew|first=Matt|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/lawson-will-linger-on-qatar-sprint-mistake-as-f1-cameo-ends/10530325/|date=8 October 2023|title=Lawson will linger on Qatar sprint mistake as F1 cameo ends|access-date=26 October 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Tsunoda wants answers over AlphaTauri issues as Lawson frustrated not to 'go out on a high' in Qatar|date=11 October 2023|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.tsunoda-wants-answers-over-alphatauri-issues-as-lawson-frustrated-not-to-go.5mQj99mgivwv65MhCSOjgC.html|access-date=26 October 2023|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> Ricciardo was well enough to return for the following {{F1 GP|2023|United States}}, and Lawson returned to his reserve role.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Ricciardo's broken hand 'a lot worse than it first seemed' as he opens up on recovery|date=19 October 2023|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.ricciardos-broken-hand-a-lot-worse-than-it-first-seemed-as-he-opens-up-on.2RtVNDB3J3JdfApvxa8S9v.html|access-date=26 October 2023|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref>

==== 2024: Late-season stint ====
Lawson continued his role as reserve driver for [[Red Bull Racing]] and [[RB Formula One Team]] (the rebranded AlphaTauri) in [[2024 Formula One World Championship|2024]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Why Lawson couldn't force his way into 2024 AlphaTauri line-up|date=23 September 2023|url=https://www.the-race.com/formula-1/alphatauri-tsunoda-ricciardo-2024-f1-news-lawson-red-bull-fate-sealed/|access-date=23 September 2023|website=The Race|language=en}}</ref> Lawson completed a filming day with the [[Red Bull RB20]] in mid-July at [[Silverstone Circuit|Silverstone]], following the [[2024 British Grand Prix|British Grand Prix]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Lawson prueba el RB20 de Red Bull F1 en Silverstone |url=https://espanol.motorsport.com/f1/news/lawson-test-red-bull-silverstone-rb20/10633900/ |website=motorsport |date=11 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240712011815/https://espanol.motorsport.com/f1/news/lawson-test-red-bull-silverstone-rb20/10633900/ |archive-date=12 July 2024 |url-status=live |last1=Faturos |first1=Federico |access-date=12 July 2024 }}</ref> At the end of July, he completed another day of testing for RB with the 2022 [[AlphaTauri AT03]] at [[Imola Circuit|Imola]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Liam Lawson hará un test con el AT03 de RB a finales de julio en Imola |url=https://soymotor.com/f1/noticias/liam-lawson-hara-un-test-con-el-at03-de-rb-finales-de-julio-en-imola |access-date=12 July 2024 |website=SoyMotor.com |language=es-ES}}</ref> Due to poor performances by Ricciardo in the early parts of the season, rumors speculated that Lawson could replace him mid-season, but Ricciardo denied later in the season that manager [[Helmut Marko]] had threatened to do so.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://racingnews365.com/ricciardo-rejects-idea-of-marko-ultimatum-over-f1-future|title=Ricciardo rejects idea of Marko ultimatum over F1 future
|website = Racingnews365|date=8 September 2024
|access-date=16 September 2024}}</ref>

Ricciardo was dropped by RB after the {{F1 GP|2024|Singapore}}, with Lawson replacing him for the remaining six rounds of the season.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/breaking-lawson-to-replace-ricciardo-at-rb-for-remainder-of-the-season.2TZRpkQAnucdPwAgudqdJ2|title=Lawson to replace Ricciardo at RB for remainder of the season|website=Formula 1.com|date=26 September 2024|access-date=26 September 2024}}</ref> At the {{F1GP|2024|United States}}, Lawson started 19th due to engine penalties.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson to start from back of the grid in Austin after being hit with engine penalty|date=18 October 2024|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/lawson-to-start-from-back-of-the-grid-in-austin-after-being-hit-with-engine.4wpMzkb9tZn1ppOzrNkMwW|access-date=9 November 2024|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> Starting on the hard tyre, Lawson went long and was able to jump teammate Tsunoda during the pit stops. His efforts yielded him ninth place, which received praise from [[Christian Horner]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Beaming Lawson 'definitely didn't' expect points on F1 return after massive grid penalty|date=21 October 2024|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/beaming-lawson-definitely-didnt-expect-points-on-f1-return-after-massive.6gztiHx6JLCTrDOBybfxZS|access-date=9 November 2024|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.si.com/fannation/racing/f1briefings/news/f1-news-liam-lawson-receives-immense-praise-from-christian-horner-amid-fight-for-red-bull-seat-01jat80gzz8m | title=F1 News: Liam Lawson Receives Immense Praise from Christian Horner Amid Fight for Red Bull Seat | access-date=24 October 2024 | archive-date=22 October 2024 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241022234149/https://www.si.com/fannation/racing/f1briefings/news/f1-news-liam-lawson-receives-immense-praise-from-christian-horner-amid-fight-for-red-bull-seat-01jat80gzz8m | url-status=dead }}</ref> Lawson qualified for the {{F1GP|2024|Mexico City}} in 12th.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2024 Mexico City Grand Prix Qualifying|date=26 October 2024|url=https://www.visacashapprb.com/int-en/2024-mexico-city-grand-prix-qualifying|access-date=9 November 2024|website=[[Visa Cashapp RB]]|language=en}}</ref> He had a contentious battle in the race with Red Bull driver [[Sergio Pérez]], and made contact with him, causing Lawson to raise his middle finger at him after passing him a few laps later. He ultimately finished one place ahead of Pérez in 16th, and later apologised as he admitted that it was "not something that [I] should have done".<ref>{{Cite web|title='I'm not just going to get out of his way' – Lawson frustrated after fierce battle with Perez in Mexico|date=28 October 2024|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/im-not-just-going-to-get-out-of-his-way-lawson-frustrated-after-fierce.7hj41bdURx7u6Kyjj5U5z8|access-date=9 November 2024|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title='It's not something I should have done' – Lawson apologises for Perez clash in Mexico but adds 'I'm not here to make friends'|date=31 October 2024|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/its-not-something-i-should-have-done-lawson-apologises-for-perez-clash-in.23WPheoPvycBLv6Bxycbx3|access-date=9 November 2024|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> After narrowly missing out on points in his first sprint at the {{F1 GP|2024|São Paulo}}, Lawson produced his best qualifying result to date in a rain-hit session, securing fifth on the grid, two places behind Tsunoda.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Tsunoda and Lawson thrilled with 'epic' qualifying result in Sao Paulo as RB look to catch Haas in constructors' battle|date=9 November 2024|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/tsunoda-and-lawson-thrilled-with-epic-qualifying-result-in-sao-paulo-as-rb.1qGX6g3cXNFWmnZ1TTzgzn|access-date=9 November 2024|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> In the race, although he was spun by [[Oscar Piastri]] on lap 26, Lawson was able to withstand the treacherous conditions and finish in ninth place.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Post Race Thoughts In Sao Paulo|date=3 November 2024|url=https://www.visacashapprb.com/int-en/2024-sao-paulo-grand-prix-race|access-date=9 November 2024|website=[[Visa Cashapp RB]]|language=en}}</ref>

At the {{F1 GP|2024|Las Vegas}}, starting 14th, Lawson attempted a one-stop strategy, but a long second stint left him pitting late and he eventually finished 16th.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Tsunoda left frustrated at 'annoying' Las Vegas race that saw Haas extend constructors' advantage over RB|date=26 November 2024|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/tsunoda-left-frustrated-at-annoying-las-vegas-race-that-saw-haas-extend.3AauOax3jspPUbFCLcDEcu|access-date=16 December 2024|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> Lawson had a miserable {{F1 GP|2024|Qatar}}, colliding with [[Valtteri Bottas]] in the opening laps which earned him a ten-second penalty. Attaining floor damage from the contact, he was left to finish in 13th place.<ref>{{Cite web|title='It wasn't my intention' – Lawson apologises for collision with Bottas in Qatar|date=3 December 2024|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/it-wasnt-my-intention-lawson-apologises-for-collision-with-bottas-in-qatar.CVMyy7Z9PNIvZ48oo9OWT|access-date=16 December 2024|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> At the {{F1 GP|2024|Abu Dhabi}}, Lawson had another troubled race when his front left wheel was not fitted correctly during his pit stop, forcing him to pit again. A mechanical issue later forced him out of the race with three laps remaining.<ref>{{Cite web|title='It sucks' – Lawson insists Abu Dhabi 'one of my better races' with tough outing ending in DNF|date=9 December 2024|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/it-sucks-lawson-insists-abu-dhabi-one-of-my-better-races-with-tough-outing.13J82AwTWGQX2e2h5GbHSs|access-date=16 December 2024|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en}}</ref> Lawson finished the season 21st in the standings, collecting four points across the six Grand Prix he competed in.

=== Red Bull Racing (2025) ===
Lawson was promoted to [[Red Bull Racing]] in {{F1|2025}} to replace [[Sergio Pérez]], partnering defending four-time World Drivers' Champion [[Max Verstappen]].<ref name="RB2025"/> At the season-opening {{F1GP|2025|Australian}}, he qualified eighteenth before crashing out of the race in changing conditions.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/articles/single-seaters/f1/rookies-face-brutal-f1-reality-the-heroes-and-zeroes-heading-into-wet-australian-gp/ |url-access=registration |title=Brutal F1 reality for rookies: qualifying bites hard ahead of wet Australian GP |last=Cooper |first=Adam |date=15 March 2025 |magazine=[[Motor Sport (magazine)|Motor Sport]] |access-date=15 March 2025 |issn=0027-2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250315182951/https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/articles/single-seaters/f1/rookies-face-brutal-f1-reality-the-heroes-and-zeroes-heading-into-wet-australian-gp/ |archive-date=15 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/articles/cr421xe109qo |title=Norris beats Verstappen in dramatic Australia opener |last=Benson |first=Andrew |date=16 March 2025 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |publisher=[[BBC]] |access-date=16 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250316070859/https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/articles/cr421xe109qo |archive-date=16 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Lawson qualified last for both the sprint and main race in [[2025 Chinese Grand Prix|China]], finishing fourteenth at the former and twelfth at the latter following three disqualifications—and a penalty for [[Jack Doohan]]—ahead of him; Red Bull opened discussions to replace him with [[Yuki Tsunoda]] after his performances in the opening two rounds.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/red-bull-considers-immediate-swap-of-lawson-and-tsunoda-for-japanese-gp/10706322/ |title=Red Bull considers immediate swap of Lawson and Tsunoda for Japanese GP |last1=Karpov
|first1=Oleg |last2=de Celis |first2=Jose Carlos |date=23 March 2025 |website=[[Autosport]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=23 March 2025 |issn=0269-946X |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250323131004/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/red-bull-considers-immediate-swap-of-lawson-and-tsunoda-for-japanese-gp/10706322/ |archive-date=23 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Return to Racing Bulls (2025–present) ===
==== 2025: Mid-season demotion ====
[[File:2025 Japan GP - Racing Bulls - Liam Lawson - FP2.jpg|thumb|left|Lawson was demoted to [[Racing Bulls]] from the {{F1GP|2025|Japanese}} onwards in {{F1|2025}}.|alt=Lawson driving the  Racing Bulls VCARB 02 at the 2025 Japanese Grand Prix]]
Lawson was replaced by [[Yuki Tsunoda]] from the {{F1GP|2025|Japanese}} onwards, with Lawson moving back to [[Racing Bulls]] to partner [[2024 Formula 2 Championship|FIA Formula 2]] runner-up [[Isack Hadjar]] for the remainder of {{F1|2025}}.<ref name="contract">{{Cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/f1/yuki-tsunoda-liam-lawson-f1-red-bull-japan-b2722436.html |title=Yuki Tsunoda replaces Liam Lawson at Red Bull for Japan GP in unprecedented F1 driver swap |last=Jackson |first=Kieran |date=27 March 2025 |work=[[The Independent]] |access-date=27 March 2025 |issn=1741-9743 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250327145829/https://www.independent.co.uk/f1/yuki-tsunoda-liam-lawson-f1-red-bull-japan-b2722436.html |archive-date=27 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Team adviser [[Helmut Marko]] admitted [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]] "made a mistake" in their decision to promote Lawson only 11 Grands Prix into his career, adding that he "lost confidence and [couldn't] show his real potential".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/articles/ckgzg0mjkero#comments |title='We made mistake' on Lawson, says Red Bull chief |last=Benson |first=Andrew |date=28 March 2025 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |publisher=[[BBC]] |access-date=28 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250328144918/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/articles/ckgzg0mjkero |archive-date=28 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He finished seventeenth on his return after a strategic error, having qualified fourteenth.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/motorsport/formula-1/formula-1-result-liam-lawson-finishes-17th-in-japan-as-tyre-strategy-costs-kiwi/V2RA5EPIG5D6HAASPETRCMBCZA/ |title=Formula 1 result: Liam Lawson finishes 17th in Japan as tyre strategy costs Kiwi, Max Verstappen wins at Suzuka |last=Powell |first=Alex |date=6 April 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |publisher=[[New Zealand Media and Entertainment]] |access-date=6 April 2025 |issn=1170-0777 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250406175858/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/motorsport/formula-1/formula-1-result-liam-lawson-finishes-17th-in-japan-as-tyre-strategy-costs-kiwi/V2RA5EPIG5D6HAASPETRCMBCZA/ |archive-date=6 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Lawson received two penalties as he finished sixteenth at the {{F1GP|2025|Bahrain}}: one for causing a collision with [[Lance Stroll]] and another with [[Nico Hülkenberg]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/motorsport/formula-1/formula-1-result-liam-lawson-penalised-twice-finishes-17th-as-oscar-piastri-wins-bahrain-grand-prix/CJ7V2L25TRH2VMGQ2Z52ZRZP6Q/ |title=Formula 1 result: Liam Lawson penalised twice, finishes 16th as Oscar Piastri wins Bahrain Grand Prix |last=Powell |first=Alex |date=13 April 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |publisher=[[New Zealand Media and Entertainment]] |access-date=15 April 2025 |issn=1170-0777 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250415161903/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/motorsport/formula-1/formula-1-result-liam-lawson-penalised-twice-finishes-17th-as-oscar-piastri-wins-bahrain-grand-prix/CJ7V2L25TRH2VMGQ2Z52ZRZP6Q/ |archive-date=15 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He finished eleventh in [[2025 Saudi Arabian Grand Prix|Saudi Arabia]], dropped to twelfth after receiving a 10-second time penalty for leaving the circuit.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/hadjar-lawson-pushing-me-like-tsunoda/10715694/ |title=Isack Hadjar: Liam Lawson "definitely pushing me" like Yuki Tsunoda used to |last=Vinel |first=Ben |date=21 April 2025 |website=[[Motorsport.com]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=21 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250421213706/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/hadjar-lawson-pushing-me-like-tsunoda/10715694/ |archive-date=21 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He finished thirteenth in the [[2025 Miami Grand Prix|Miami]] sprint after receiving a five-second penalty for a collision with [[Fernando Alonso]],<ref>{{cite web |url=https://racingnews365.com/charles-leclerc-crashes-out-before-sprint-as-heavy-rain-causes-chaos |title=Charles Leclerc crashes out before Sprint as heavy rain causes chaos |date=3 May 2025 |last=Golding |first=Nick |website=RacingNews365}}</ref> and retired during the main race due to [[Ground effect (cars)|floor]] damage.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/piastri-wins-from-norris-and-russell-as-mclaren-seal-commanding-1-2-in-miami.6Vfaf5zEMOKmPHzHdkGdof|title=Piastri wins from Norris and Russell as McLaren seal commanding 1-2 in Miami Grand Prix|website=Formula 1.com|date=5 May 2025}}</ref> He scored his first points of the season at the {{F1GP|2025|Monaco}}, where he played a key role in helping teammate Hadjar finish sixth, as he claimed eighth. He scored points again at the {{F1GP|2025|Austrian}} in a career-best sixth. He sustained terminal damage after colliding with [[Esteban Ocon]] on the opening lap of the {{F1GP|2025|British}}. Lawson finished eighth at both the [[2025 Belgian Grand Prix|Belgian]] and [[2025 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungarian]] Grands Prix, holding Max Verstappen behind him at the latter. Lawson broke both his qualifying and race finish records at the [[2025 Azerbaijan Grand Prix|Azerbaijan Grand Prix]], qualifying third and finishing fifth. Lawson finished 15th at the [[2025 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore Grand Prix]], remaining 9 points ahead of Hadjar in the drivers' standings.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wilson |first=Chris |date=9 October 2025 |title=Liam Lawson Reflects on Racing Against Max Verstappen Before Being Fired |url=https://formula1news.co.uk/liam-lawson-reflects-on-racing-against-max-verstappen-before-being-fired/ |access-date=12 October 2025 |website=Formula1News.co.uk |language=en-GB}}</ref>

==== 2026: New regulations ====
[[File:2026 Chinese GP - Racing Bulls - Liam Lawson - Qualifying.jpg|right|thumb|Lawson at the [[2026 Chinese Grand Prix]]]]
Lawson is contracted to remain at Racing Bulls in {{F1|2026}}, partnered by rookie [[Arvid Lindblad]].<ref name="2026contract">{{Cite web |url=https://www.the-race.com/formula-1/liam-lawson-interview-new-racing-bulls-deal-2026-f1/ |title=The Norris-like change that helped Lawson save his F1 seat |last=Noble |first=Jon |date=3 December 2025 |website=The Race |publisher=The Race Media |access-date=4 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251204154948/https://www.the-race.com/formula-1/liam-lawson-interview-new-racing-bulls-deal-2026-f1/ |archive-date=4 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> In the [[2026 Chinese Grand Prix|second round of the 2026 season]] he brought home 8 points by finishing in 7th place in both the sprint and main race for his first points of the season.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Powell |first=Alex |title=Lawson takes seventh in Chinese Grand Prix, completes double points weekend |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/motorsport/formula-1/formula-1-result-liam-lawson-completes-double-points-race-weekend-takes-seventh-in-chinese-grand-prix/ZXAFCLFSP5EUZHJH5LN3ATUTWU/ |access-date=2026-05-25 |website=NZ Herald |language=en-NZ}}</ref> He continued this strong form taking 9th in the [[2026 Japanese Grand Prix|Japanese Grand Prix]], and following a DNF in Miami he was able to finish 7th in [[2026 Canadian Grand Prix|Canada]] equalling his best result of the season.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Waikato Times |url=https://www.waikatotimes.co.nz/sport/361010365/liam-lawson-seventh-kimi-antonelli-wins-canadian-grand-prix |access-date=2026-05-25 |website=www.waikatotimes.co.nz}}</ref> At the subsequent [[2026 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco Grand Prix]], he initially equalled his career best result in Formula One achieving a 5th place finish.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lawson and Lindblad praise 'big turnaround' by Racing Bulls |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/a-big-turnaround-lawson-and-lindblad-praise-racing-bulls-performance-after-monaco-double-points-finish.kcmkqXQQfKKhcUG2fYaey |access-date=2026-06-10 |website=www.formula1.com |language=en}}</ref> However [[Pierre Gasly]] was later reinstated onto the podium after his penalties were successfully appealed and removed, dropping Lawson to 6th.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McLaughlin |first=Luke |date=2026-06-12 |title=Gasly reinstated to Monaco podium after F1 officials admit timekeeping blunder |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jun/12/gasly-reinstated-monaco-gp-podium-f1-timekeeping-blunder |access-date=2026-06-12 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Benson |first=Andrew |date=2026-06-12 |title=Monaco Grand Prix: Pierre Gasly regains third place after Alpine successfully appeal against penalties |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/articles/cevlg742y3do |access-date=2026-06-12 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref> Lawson continued to score points at the next three races, with an eighth place finish at [[2026 Barcelona-Catalunya Grand Prix|Barcelona-Catalunya]], a ninth place finish in [[2026 Austrian Grand Prix|Austria]], and a sixth place finish at [[2026 British Grand Prix|Silverstone]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Barcelona-Catalunya 2026 - Result • STATS F1 |url=https://www.statsf1.com/en/2026/barcelone-catalogne/classement.aspx |access-date=2026-07-19 |website=www.statsf1.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Austria 2026 - Result • STATS F1 |url=https://www.statsf1.com/en/2026/autriche/classement.aspx |access-date=2026-07-19 |website=www.statsf1.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Britain 2026 - Result • STATS F1 |url=https://www.statsf1.com/en/2026/grande-bretagne/classement.aspx |access-date=2026-07-19 |website=www.statsf1.com}}</ref> He placed eighth again at the [[2026 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungarian Grand Prix]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-07-26 |title=F1 2026 Hungarian Grand Prix results |url=https://www.the-race.com/formula-1/f1-2026-hungarian-grand-prix-results/ |access-date=2026-07-26 |website=The Race |language=en}}</ref> 

Lawson has returned to Red Bull Racing for the [[2026 Dutch Grand Prix|Dutch Grand Prix]] replacing Hadjar who broke his wrist during the summer break.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 August 2026 |title=Hadjar to miss Dutch GP as Lawson steps up |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/red-bull-confirm-hadjar-to-miss-dutch-grand-prix-as-lawson-steps-up-and-tsunoda-returns.3D3k7oc9dUwwEBZC7upjR1 |access-date=19 August 2026 |website=[[Formula 1]] |language=}}</ref>

== Other racing ==
=== Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters (2021) ===
Lawson competed in the [[2021 Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters|Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters]] (DTM) in 2021, driving a [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]]-sponsored [[Ferrari 488 GT3]] for [[AF Corse]] alongside [[Formula One]] reserve driver [[Alex Albon]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson to race Red Bull Ferrari|date=3 February 2021|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-to-race-red-bull-ferrari/|access-date=16 July 2023|website=liamlawson.co.nz|language=en|archive-date=16 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230716034709/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-to-race-red-bull-ferrari/|url-status=dead}}</ref> He competed in the championship concurrently with his rookie Formula 2 campaign. Lawson made an immediate impact on his debut in [[Autodromo Nazionale di Monza|Monza]], winning the opening race. In doing so, he became the youngest-ever race winner in the DTM history.<ref>{{cite web|date=19 June 2021|title=Ferrari triumphs at home track in maiden DTM race|url=https://www.dtm.com/en/news/ferrari-triumphs-at-home-track-in-maiden-dtm-race|access-date=4 September 2021|website=DTM.com|language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson makes history at Monza!|date=20 July 2021|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-makes-history-at-monza/|access-date=16 July 2023|website=liamlawson30.com|language=en|archive-date=16 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230716040215/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-makes-history-at-monza/|url-status=dead}}</ref> After a spin in the second Monza race costed him a points finish, he finished second in both races at the [[EuroSpeedway Lausitz|Lausitzring]], leading the second race before losing the win due to an issue during his pit stop.<ref>{{cite web|title=Lawson takes blame for slow pitstop, losing DTM race lead|url=https://www.motorsport.com/dtm/news/lawson-pitstop-error-lausitzring-red-bull/6636963/|website=Motorsport.com|language=en-GB|author=Thukral, Rachit|date=26 July 2021|access-date=4 September 2021}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Double podium for Lawson in Germany|date=26 July 2021|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/double-podium-for-lawson-in-germany/|access-date=16 July 2023|website=liamlawson.co.nz|language=en|archive-date=16 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230716180444/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/double-podium-for-lawson-in-germany/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Following a challenging mid-season run in which he failed to score points in three of the next four races,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson positive heading to the Red Bull Ring|date=2 September 2021|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-positive-heading-to-the-red-bull-ring/|access-date=16 July 2023|website=liamlawson.co.nz|language=en|archive-date=16 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230716034709/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-positive-heading-to-the-red-bull-ring/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Lawson returned to winning form at the [[Red Bull Ring]]. He secured his first pole position and later converted into his second victory during the opening race.<ref>{{cite web|title=Red Bull Ring DTM: Lawson reignites title challenge with win|url=https://www.motorsport.com/dtm/news/red-bull-ring-lawson-win-ferrari/6660132/|website=[[Motorsport.com]]|language=en-GB|author=Thukral, Rachit|date=4 September 2021|access-date=4 September 2021}}</ref> The following day, Lawson claimed another win after starting second, strengthening his title challenge.<ref>{{cite web|title=Red Bull Ring DTM: Lawson holds off Wittmann for double win|url=https://www.motorsport.com/dtm/news/red-bull-ring-lawson-win-wittmann/6660982/|website=Motorsport.com|language=en-GB|author=Thukral, Rachit|date=5 September 2021|access-date=5 September 2021}}</ref>

Lawson continued to score consistently in the latter stages of the season, recording four podium finishes and a fourth-place finish over the next five races,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Double podium puts Lawson on top|date=20 September 2021|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/double-podium-puts-lawson-on-top/|access-date=16 July 2023|website=liamlawson30.com|language=en|archive-date=16 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230716181946/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/double-podium-puts-lawson-on-top/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson leads into DTM final|date=4 October 2021|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-leads-into-dtm-final/|access-date=16 July 2023|website=liamlawson30.com|language=en|archive-date=16 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230716181958/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-leads-into-dtm-final/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Heading into the final race at the [[Norisring]], he qualified on pole position and led the drivers' championship by 19 points over [[Kelvin van der Linde]], with [[Maximilian Götz]] a further three points adrift of van der Linde.<ref>{{cite web|title=Norisring DTM: Lawson beats van der Linde to pole for finale|url=https://www.motorsport.com/dtm/news/norisring-lawson-pole-van-der-linde/6684298/|website=Motorsport.com|language=en-GB|author=Thukral, Rachit|date=10 October 2021|access-date=10 October 2021}}</ref> However, Lawson's championship unravelled at the start when contact with van der Linde on the opening lap damaged his car, which left him lapping significantly off the pace after rejoining. Furthermore, Mercedes team orders to [[Lucas Auer]] and [[Philip Ellis (racing driver)|Philip Ellis]] enabled Götz to move into the lead and secure the victory, putting him three points ahead of Lawson.<ref>{{cite web|title=Norisring DTM: Gotz wins title after Mercedes imposes team orders|url=https://www.motorsport.com/dtm/news/norisring-gotz-mercedes-team-orders-lawson/6684404/|website=Motorsport.com|language=en-GB|author=Thukral, Rachit|date=10 October 2021|access-date=10 October 2021}}</ref> Van der Linde was later given a five-second penalty for his involvement in the opening-lap incident.<ref>{{cite web|title=Lawson hits out at "idiot" van der Linde after losing DTM title|url=https://www.motorsport.com/dtm/news/lawson-idiot-van-der-linde-title-norisring/6685004/|website=Motorsport.com|language=en-GB|author1=Thukral, Rachit|author2=Haidinger, Sven|date=11 October 2021|access-date=11 October 2021}}</ref> Lawson finished the season runner-up in the drivers' championship. Following the conclusion of the campaign, he expressed his dissatisfaction of the title outcome, stating that van der Linde was "the dirtiest guy [he's] ever raced against." Lawson also stated that he no longer intended to remain in the series.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Thukral|first=Rachit|url=https://www.motorsport.com/dtm/news/red-bulls-lawson-no-longer-wants-to-be-part-of-dtm/6686024/|date=14 October 2021|title=Red Bull's Lawson no longer wants to be part of DTM|access-date=16 July 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref>

=== Super Formula (2023) ===
Lawson left Formula 2 at the end of 2022 and contested the [[2023 Super Formula Championship]] with reigning champions [[Mugen Motorsports|Team Mugen]] alongside two-time drivers' champion [[Tomoki Nojiri]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=James|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-red-bull-mugen-2023/10396976/|date=9 November 2022|title=Red Bull junior Lawson set for Super Formula move|access-date=9 November 2022|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> He made an immediate impression, qualifying third for his series debut in [[Fuji Speedway|Fuji]] and becoming the first driver to win on their Japanese Top Formula debut since 1978.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-relieved-fuji-qualifying-mugen/10454263/|date=8 April 2023|title=Lawson "relieved" after maiden Super Formula qualifying|access-date=6 May 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-fuji-safety-car-scare/10454343/|date=8 April 2023|title=Lawson reveals safety car scare on way to Super Formula win|access-date=6 May 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=11 April 2023 |title=How Red Bull's latest F1 hopeful Lawson made a perfect start in Japan |url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/how-red-bulls-latest-f1-hopeful-lawson-made-a-perfect-start-in-japan/10455070/ |access-date=25 August 2023 |website=www.motorsport.com |language=en}}</ref> In the second race, Lawson ran in third, but a safety car infringement penalty demoted him to fifth.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-fuji-penalty-safety-car/10454550/|date=9 April 2023|title=Lawson questions penalty, says pitstop rules "frustrating"|access-date=6 May 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Lawson wins Fuji Super Formula debut|date=11 April 2023|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-wins-fuji-super-formula-debut/|access-date=6 May 2023|website=liamlawson.co.nz|language=en|archive-date=6 May 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230506093446/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/lawson-wins-fuji-super-formula-debut/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In [[Suzuka Circuit|Suzuka]], he qualified ninth<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-suzuka-qualifying-disappointed-nojiri/10459279/|date=22 April 2023|title=Lawson "very disappointed" with Suzuka qualifying performance|access-date=6 May 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> but recovered strongly to finish fourth.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Fourth for Lawson in Suzuka|date=24 April 2023|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/fourth-for-lawson-at-suzuka/|access-date=6 May 2023|website=liamlawson.co.nz|language=en|archive-date=2 December 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231202120848/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/fourth-for-lawson-at-suzuka/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In [[Autopolis]], he qualified second while teammate Nojiri was sidelined due to a collapsed lung,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-respect-nojiri-unwell-autopolis/10471441/|date=20 May 2023|title=Lawson has "huge respect" for unwell Nojiri coming to track|access-date=23 May 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> Lawson managed to undercut polesitter [[Sho Tsuboi]] during the pit cycle and resisted a late charge from [[Ritomo Miyata]] to secure the win, moving him into the championship lead.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-not-thinking-about-f1-rumours-after-latest-super-formula-win/10471760/|date=21 May 2023|title=Lawson not thinking about F1 rumours after latest Super Formula win|access-date=23 May 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Another win puts Lawson in the lead of Super Formula|date=22 May 2023|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/another-win-puts-lawson-in-the-lead-of-super-formula/|access-date=23 May 2023|website=liamlawson.co.nz|language=en|archive-date=16 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230716181944/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/another-win-puts-lawson-in-the-lead-of-super-formula/|url-status=dead}}</ref> A more difficult weekend followed in [[Sportsland SUGO|Sugo]], qualifying sixth.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/nojiri-sugo-mugen-return-lawson/10483925/|date=17 June 2023|title=Nojiri still not "at 100%" at Sugo after collapsed lung|access-date=21 June 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> A radio miscommunication during the race limited Lawson to fifth, allowing Miyata to take a 12-point lead in the standings.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-sugo-mugen-miscommunication-strategy/10485048/|date=17 June 2023|title=The miscommunication that cost Lawson dearly at Sugo|access-date=21 June 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> Lawson responded decisively in the second Fuji round, claiming his second win and closing the championship deficit to a single point.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Fuji double for Lawson|date=17 July 2023|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/fuji-double-for-lawson/|access-date=18 July 2023|website=liamlawson.co.nz|language=en|archive-date=18 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230718122219/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/fuji-double-for-lawson/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-fuji-victory-not-confident/10496474/|date=17 July 2023|title=Lawson "wasn't confident at all" about Fuji victory chances|access-date=18 July 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref>

In [[Twin Ring Motegi|Motegi]], Lawson qualified third, scoring a point and briefly taking the championship lead.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-motegi-qualifying-worries-miyata/10509050/|date=19 August 2023|title=Lawson "grateful" to qualify P3 after Motegi Q2 worries|access-date=21 August 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> However, he spun at the while attempting to pass Nojiri, triggering a multi-car pileup which red-flagged the race.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Thukral|first=Rachit|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/motegi-super-formula-race-red-flagged-after-lawson-spin/10509382/|date=20 August 2023|title=Motegi Super Formula race stopped as Lawson spin causes crash|access-date=21 August 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> A subsequent drive-through penalty for work carried out on his car under red-flag conditions left him classified thirteenth.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Disaster at Motegi for Lawson|date=21 August 2023|url=https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/disaster-at-motegi-for-lawson/|access-date=21 August 2023|website=liamlawson.co.nz|language=en|archive-date=21 August 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230821122729/https://www.liamlawson.co.nz/news/disaster-at-motegi-for-lawson/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Lawson praised his team's efforts in repairing his car in time for the restart.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-mugen-rebuild-motegi-crash/10509767/|date=21 August 2023|title=Lawson 'amazed' by rapid Mugen rebuild after Motegi crash|access-date=21 August 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> Heading into the [[Suzuka Circuit|Suzuka double-header finale]], Lawson trailed Miyata by eight points. He qualified seventh for the opening race<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/suzuka-qualifying-nojiri-pole-lawson/10538955/|date=28 October 2023|title=Suzuka Super Formula: Nojiri takes vital pole, Lawson P7|access-date=7 November 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> but an early red flag confined him to sixth.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Thukral|first=Rachit|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-laments-red-flag-suzuka-title-hopes/10539165/|date=28 October 2023|title=Lawson laments Suzuka red flag as Super Formula title hopes fade|access-date=7 November 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> Lawson then took his first and only pole of the season for the second race.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/suzuka-lawson-pole-miyata-nojiri/10539643/|date=29 October 2023|title=Suzuka Super Formula: Lawson keeps title hopes alive with pole|access-date=7 November 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> He lost the lead to [[Kakunoshin Ota]] at the start and ultimately finished second.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Thukral|first=Rachit|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/suzuka-super-formula-miyata-beats-lawson-to-title-ota-wins-finale/10539716/|date=29 October 2023|title=Suzuka Super Formula: Miyata beats Lawson to title, Ota wins finale|access-date=7 November 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref> Lawson concluded the season runner-up in the standings recording one pole, three wins, four podiums and 106.5 points.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/lawson-consistency-title-miyata-suzuka/10539875/|date=29 October 2023|title=Lawson admits he lacked consistency for Super Formula title|access-date=7 November 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref>

Lawson did not continue in Super Formula for [[2024 Super Formula Championship|2024]], opting to focus full-time on his reserve driver commitments, and was replaced in his seat by [[Ayumu Iwasa]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Jamie|url=https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/hirakawa-quits-toyota-miyata-carlin/10549973/|date=22 November 2023|title=Hirakawa joins Miyata, Lawson in Super Formula exodus|access-date=22 November 2023|website=[[Motorsport.com]]}}</ref>

